        "original_id": "21f730e5-a241-568c-b7d9-9d66e411b109",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Concepts: Grassmann's law, dissimilation, aspiration\n- People: Hermann Grassmann\n- Languages: Ancient Greek, Sanskrit\n- Roles: linguist, mathematician, polymath\n\nFacts:\n- Hermann Günther Grassmann was born on April 15, 1809, and died on September 26, 1877.\n- Hermann Grassmann was a German polymath who was known in his own day as a linguist and is now also known as a mathematician.\n- Grassmann's law is named after its discoverer Hermann Grassmann.\n- Grassmann's law is a dissimilatory phonological process in Ancient Greek and Sanskrit.\n- Grassmann's law states that when an aspirated consonant is followed by another aspirated consonant in the next syllable, the first consonant loses its aspiration.",
